  "description": "Seal key and seal-key document (spec §3.6). seal_keys: SealSK[e] = HKDF-SHA256(ikm=VK[e], salt=b64decode(vault_id), info=utf8(\"akv1/seal-key\\n\") || cjson({\"key_epoch\":e}), L=32), used unmodified as an X25519 secret; seal_pub = X25519(SealSK, 9). Documents are signed with context \"akv1/seal-key\" over the document without sig. verifySealKeyDoc(doc, chain, {vault_id, vk?, trusted_anchor?, witnessed_checkpoint?}) runs against membership.json chains[0] (the shared chain, verified in full) and checks in order: shape (exact keys; v = 1 else unsupported_version; IDs, 32-byte seal_pub, integers: bad_request); vault_id is the expected vault (bad_request); membership_seq <= verified head seq and key_epoch = the chain key epoch at membership_seq (bad_request); author is a human owner device current at membership_seq, never the recovery key (forbidden); signature under the author sign_pub (invalid_signature); with vk (null = not held), seal_pub = seal_pub recomputed from vk (forbidden). A case with trusted_anchor or witnessed_checkpoint verifies the chain with those pins as membership.json chains do (anchor mismatch: forbidden; a shorter or forked chain: rollback), and the prefix up to membership_seq with the anchor only. cases MUST verify; invalid MUST fail with expect_error. replace: the server keeps one document per (vault_id, key_epoch) and refuses an incoming document whose membership_seq is below the stored one (conflict); replace_cases MUST be accepted, replace_invalid MUST fail.",
